Here is the main point that many people don't understand... People ask 
for help with combat, because they aren't good at combat.  You can't 
say the people asking for help are good at melee combat, because if 
they were good at it, then they wouldn't have asked for help with 
combat.

Again, I'm going to say that there are many times where someone dies 
in melee range of a monster, that is not due to the fact that they 
didn't do enough ranged combat.  Again, I'll bring up the case with 
ants, where if someone died from being surrounded by ants, then they 
should be told to avoid being surrounded (i.e. run to a chokepoint or 
engrave Elebereth).  Telling them to use more ranged combat won't 
help.

Another case involves hostile warhorses.  If someone dies to a hostile 
warhorse, it is not due to the fact that they didn't do enough ranged 
combat.  Instead, they should have throw food at the warhorse so that 
they could have avoided the combat altogether.

Encouraging people to do large amounts of ranged combat, encourages 
them to overlook their other combat options.  There are many cases 
where ranged combat isn't the best solution, so we shouldn't be 
promoting it as a rule of thumb.  For example, if I see a vampire lord 
in the bottom of the mines, I think the best solution is to avoid it 
completely (using Elebereth if it gets too close).  But if an 
unexperienced player sees Jove's massive damage tables, and Jove's 
constant suggestions to "throw daggers early and often", then it's 
very likely they'll think they should throw daggers at the vampire 
lord.  This will probably result in the vampire lord still having a 
large amount of health after the player's entire stack of daggers was 
throw at it.

The whole focus on ranged damaged is terrible, because it causes many 
people to use ranged combat as a crutch.  Sure it will probably allow 
them to survive longer, which gives the illusion that it's a better 
mindset, but it will probably just result in a later YASD, which 
happens when they finally get into a situation they can't solve by 
throwing daggers.  People should be encouraged to evaluate every 
situation on a case by case basis, which they can't properly do if 
they've been convinced that one option is overwhelmingly better than 
the others.

Trash mobs are not a fantasy.  There really are a lot of monsters in 
the game which aren't a serious threat to classes built for melee 
combat.  Throwing projectiles at these monsters to increase that 
weapon skill is ok.  However, throwing projectiles at these monsters 
when you can't increase that weapon skill really isn't going to 
increase your overall survival rate by any noticable amount.  Yes, 
there might be special cases where you are low on health and you need 
to avoid any damage at all, but it most cases the tiny damage done by 
the trash mobs really isn't putting your character in any danger.



